I'll solve this using orthocentric coordinates.

## 证明

**Setup.** Place the orthocenter $H$ at the origin. Let $\mathbf a, \mathbf b, \mathbf c$ be the position vectors of $A, B, C$. The orthocenter condition gives
$$\mathbf a\cdot(\mathbf b-\mathbf c)=0,\quad \mathbf b\cdot(\mathbf a-\mathbf c)=0,\quad \mathbf c\cdot(\mathbf a-\mathbf b)=0,$$
so all pairwise dot products are equal; denote $\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b=\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf c=\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf c=s$. Set $A=|\mathbf a|^2,\ B=|\mathbf b|^2,\ C=|\mathbf c|^2$.

**Circumcenter.** The circumcenter $O$ (with $H$ as origin) is $O=\tfrac12(\mathbf a+\mathbf b+\mathbf c)$. Indeed $2O\cdot(\mathbf b-\mathbf a)=(\mathbf a+\mathbf b+\mathbf c)\cdot(\mathbf b-\mathbf a)=B-A$, and similarly $2O\cdot(\mathbf c-\mathbf a)=C-A$, so $O$ is equidistant from $A,B,C$.

**Feet of the altitudes.** Since $A_1$ lies on $BC$ and on line $HA$ (the altitude through $H$), write $A_1=\lambda\mathbf a=(1-t)\mathbf b+t\mathbf c$. Dotting with $\mathbf a$ gives $\lambda A=s$, so
$$A_1=\frac{s}{A}\mathbf a,\qquad B_1=\frac{s}{B}\mathbf b,\qquad C_1=\frac{s}{C}\mathbf c.$$

**The point $C_2=AB\cap A_1B_1$.** A point on $AB$ has the form $(1-u)\mathbf a+u\mathbf b$; a point on $A_1B_1$ has the form $(1-v)\frac{s}{A}\mathbf a+v\frac{s}{B}\mathbf b$. Equating coefficients and using $u=v\,s/B$ gives $u=\dfrac{A-s}{A-B}$, hence
$$C_2=\frac{(s-B)\mathbf a+(A-s)\mathbf b}{A-B}.$$

**Equation of $\ell_C$.** The direction of $CC_2$ is (up to scalar)
$$\mathbf d_C=(s-B)\mathbf a+(A-s)\mathbf b-(A-B)\mathbf c.$$
The line $\ell_C$ passes through the midpoint $\mathbf c/2$ of $CH$ and is perpendicular to $\mathbf d_C$, so a point $\mathbf X$ lies on $\ell_C$ iff
$$\mathbf X\cdot\mathbf d_C=\tfrac12\,\mathbf c\cdot\mathbf d_C.$$
Compute the right-hand side:
$$\mathbf c\cdot\mathbf d_C=(s-B)(\mathbf c\cdot\mathbf a)+(A-s)(\mathbf c\cdot\mathbf b)-(A-B)|\mathbf c|^2=(s-B)s+(A-s)s-(A-B)C=(A-B)(s-C).$$
Thus $\ell_C:\ \mathbf X\cdot\mathbf d_C=\dfrac{(A-B)(s-C)}{2}$.

**$O$ lies on $\ell_C$.** We evaluate $(\mathbf a+\mathbf b+\mathbf c)\cdot\mathbf d_C$ term by term:
- $\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf d_C=(s-B)A+(A-s)s-(A-B)s=(s-B)(A-s)$,
- $\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf d_C=(s-B)s+(A-s)B-(A-B)s=s^2+AB-s(A+B)$,
- $\mathbf c\cdot\mathbf d_C=(A-B)(s-C)$.

Summing:
$$(s-B)(A-s)+\bigl(s^2+AB-s(A+B)\bigr)+(A-B)(s-C).$$
Expanding $(s-B)(A-s)=sA-s^2-AB+sB$ and $(A-B)(s-C)=sA-AC-sB+BC$, the total is
$$sA-s^2-AB+sB+s^2+AB-sA-sB+sA-AC-sB+BC=(A-B)(s-C).$$
Therefore
$$O\cdot\mathbf d_C=\tfrac12(\mathbf a+\mathbf b+\mathbf c)\cdot\mathbf d_C=\frac{(A-B)(s-C)}{2},$$
which is exactly the defining equation of $\ell_C$. Hence $O\in\ell_C$.

**Cyclic symmetry.** The construction of $\ell_A$ (using $A_2=BC\cap B_1C_1$, midpoint of $AH$, perpendicular to $AA_2$) and of $\ell_B$ are obtained from that of $\ell_C$ by the cyclic relabeling $(A,B,C)\mapsto(B,C,A)$ (resp. $(C,A,B)$). The orthocentric coordinate setup, the formula $O=\tfrac12(\mathbf a+\mathbf b+\mathbf c)$, and the computation above are all invariant under this cyclic permutation. Therefore the identical argument gives $O\in\ell_A$ and $O\in\ell_B$.

**Conclusion.** All three lines $\ell_A,\ell_B,\ell_C$ pass through the circumcenter $O$, so they are concurrent. $\quad\blacksquare$

